#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153457 :tutorial/content "Recall that the residue class of $a$ modulo $n$, denoted $[a]_n$, is the set of integers that are congruent to $a$ modulo $n$.\n\n$$\n[a]_n = \\big\\{x \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: \\mid \\: x \\equiv a \\, \\: (\\text{mod}\\:n) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- The residue class of $0$ modulo $2$ corresponds to the even integers because all even integers have a remainder of $0$ when divided by $2$.\n$[0]_{2} = {0, ± 2, ± 4, …}$\n- On the other hand, the residue class of $1$ modulo $2$ corresponds to the odd integers because all odd integers have a remainder of $1$ when divided by $2$.\n$[1]_{2} = {± 1, ± 3, ± 5, …}$\n\nNotice that the residue classes $[0]_2$ and $[1]_2$ *partition* the integers, meaning that any integer belongs either to $[0]_2$ or $[1]_2$, but never both.\n\n![](images/89/8932542ec22de980b3b2e1a5a11a3edf2250efc7ea4f1ca64ca35aa4031c63c9.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "33bb31fa-3552-5d74-b6fc-efc1bc68b367" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9029 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153458 :tutorial/content "A residue class is a particular type of a more general object called an **equivalence class.**\n\nGiven an equivalence relation $\\sim$ on a set $A$, the equivalence class of an element $a \\in A$, denoted $[a]$, consists of all the elements of $A$ that are equivalent to $a$.\n\n$$\n[a] = \\{x \\in A \\, \\mid \\, x \\sim a \\}\n$$\n\nSince any equivalence relation is symmetric, we can also write\n\n$$\n[a] = \\{x \\in A \\, \\mid \\, a \\sim x \\}\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the equivalence relation $\\sim$ on $\\mathbb{Z}$ defined by $a \\sim b$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a &= & b\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUnder this relation, two integers are equivalent if they have the same absolute value. The first few equivalence classes are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[0] &= {a ∈ Z∣a∼0} \\\\\n= {a ∈ Z∣\\begin{vmatrix}a &= & 0\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\\n= {a ∈ Z∣\\begin{vmatrix}a &= 0} \\\\ = {0} \\\\ [1]|= {a ∈ Z∣a∼1} \\\\ = {a ∈ Z∣\\begin{vmatrix}a|= & 1\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\ = {a ∈ Z∣ \\mid a|= 1} \\\\ = {± 1} \\\\ [2]|= {a ∈ Z∣a∼2} \\\\ = {a ∈ Z∣\\begin{vmatrix}a|= & 2\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\ = {a ∈ Z∣ \\mid a|= 2} \\\\ = {± 2} \\\\ [3]|= {a ∈ Z∣a∼3} \\\\ = {a ∈ Z∣\\begin{vmatrix}a|= & 3\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\ = {a ∈ Z∣\\end{vmatrix}a|= 3} \\\\\n&= {± 3} \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Equivalence relations can have a finite or infinite number of equivalence classes. This particular equivalence relation has an infinite number of equivalence classes.\n- For equivalence classes containing more than one element, we can use any element to represent the class. For example, $[1]$ and $[-1]$ represent the same equivalence class." :tutorial/id #uuid "67a56179-d17e-54ab-86a3-aeca86ce5a7d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9030 :tutorial/title "Equivalence Classes"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153480 :tutorial/content "The properties of residue classes generalize to equivalence classes. Let's list some of them:\n\n- If $a \\sim b$ then $[a]=[b]$. This allows us to pick any element of a class as its representative.\n- On the other hand, two distinct equivalence classes have an empty intersection:\n$a \\not\\sim b \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad [a] \\cap [b] = \\emptyset$\n- The residue classes form a partition of $A$. Moreover, $A$ can be constructed from the (disjoint) union of all distinct equivalence classes:\n$A = \\bigcup_{i\\in I}\\,[a]_i$\nwhere $[a]_i$ denotes the $i$'th residue class and $I$ is an index set spanning all possible classes.\n\nConsider once again the equivalence relation $\\sim$ on $\\mathbb{Z}$ defined by $a \\sim b$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a &= & b\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe found that the equivalence classes were as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[0] &= {0} \\\\\n[1] &= {± 1} \\\\\n[2] &= {± 2} \\\\\n[3] &= {± 3} \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese equivalence classes form a partition of the set of integers:\n$\\big\\{[0], [1], [2],\\ldots\\big\\}$\n\nMoreover, the integers can be constructed from the disjoint union of these classes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nZ &= [0]∪[1]∪[2]∪⋯ \\\\\n&= {0}∪{± 1}∪{± 2}∪⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6f51d5c2-6cda-51cd-9f6f-70d3f573d73b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9358 :tutorial/title "Properties of Equivalence Classes"}]}
